  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Los Angeles Dodgers announced themselves early and never looked back, finishing the regular season at 100-62 with a run differential of plus-201 that ranked among the best in baseball. From Will Smith's opening-week two-run blast to a dominant September surge, this was a team built to peak in October — and for the most part, they delivered. The Dodgers led the NL West wire-to-wire, powered by a balanced attack that produced 204 home runs, an ERA of 3.55, and contributions up and down a roster that seemed to have an answer for every challenge.\n\nThe early months established the tone. A 4-1 March gave way to a productive April and a 18-10 May, with clutch hits from Kyle Tucker, Shohei Ohtani, and Max Muncy fueling the charge. The bullpen, particularly Tanner Scott, had its wobbles — surrendering key home runs in late May and early June — but the offense kept responding, none more dramatically than Dalton Rushing's two-RBI single on June 19th that swung a critical game by nearly 0.83 in win probability, one of the biggest individual moments of the entire season.\n\nAugust brought the only real turbulence of the year, as the Dodgers stumbled to a 13-14 mark. Edwin Díaz was touched for a pair of brutal outings in back-to-back games, and the bullpen collectively struggled. Yet even in the lean stretch, the offense kept manufacturing moments — Muncy's three-run homer on August 13th, Tommy Edman's clutch single on August 21st — that kept the team from any prolonged freefall. September was a revelation: an 18-7 record, capped by Teoscar Hernández, Mookie Betts, and Tommy Edman all delivering thunderous home runs that seemed to signal a team rounding into form at exactly the right moment.\n\nThe postseason has been a different, tighter beast. The Dodgers dispatched their Division Series opponent in convincing fashion, with contributions across the lineup — Tucker, Freeman, Enrique Hernández, and Andy Pages all delivering big moments. Now, down 0-1 in the NLCS, Los Angeles faces its first real test of October resolve. The first clip of the championship series ends with Mookie Betts flying out to center field, a small but symbolic reminder that nothing in October comes easy. Whether this club can flip the series and march toward a World Series title remains to be written.",
